description:
`app/static/css/shell.css` hides the Feedback control below 980 pixels:

    @media (max-width: 980px) {
      ...
      .feedbackButton { display:none; }
      ...
    }

`#feedbackButton` is the only way into the feedback dialog — `main.js` wires `openFeedback` to it and to nothing else. So at 390 pixels a steward cannot drop feedback at all.

Found while writing converge-719's offline tests: driving the feedback write from the screen at 390 timed out with Playwright reporting "element is not visible" against a button that is present in the DOM. Steer was used instead, because it is the one of the five writes reachable from the screen at both widths. Measured 2026-09-04.

platform-web.v1 Core 1 says all five writes work; §4 (IDIOM) says the phone and the laptop "show the same places, the same list, and the same words, and differ in arrangement only". A control that exists on one and not the other takes a behavior away, and §3 says that is the moment an idiom becomes a divergence.

The neighbouring rules in the same block show the intended shape — `.icon-label-button span { display:none }` with an `::after` label, and `#consoleToggle { display:flex }` kept explicitly at ≤520px for exactly this reason — so the likely fix is to shrink the control rather than remove it.

Owner: whoever owns `app/static/css/shell.css` and the topbar. The converge-719 lane owns only the offline banner rule in that file, so this is written down rather than made.

Found while finishing converge-are on 2026-09-04, driving a real Chromium against the running app at 390x844.

`app/static/css/console.css` makes the Manager Console a fixed sheet on a phone:

    @media (max-width: 980px) { .manager-console { position:fixed; z-index:50; left:10px; right:10px; bottom:10px; height:min(68vh,650px); } }
    @media (max-width: 520px) { .manager-console { left:6px; right:6px; bottom:6px; height:76vh; } }

and `app/static/js/state.js` opens it by default (`consoleOpen: true`). At 390x844 that sheet covers everything below roughly y=197 — which is most of Home, including the list of manager sessions itself.

Measured: a click on Home's tell-all control (rect [16,418,211,457]) is refused with `#consoleBody` from `<aside id="managerConsole">` intercepting pointer events. The same is true of the `.home-manager-card` buttons, which are the list. So on arrival on a phone, `experience.v1` Core 1's "Home is the list of manager sessions you run" is drawn but not touchable until the steward pushes the sheet down.

`platform-web.v1` §6 says the console is a sheet you "pull up when you want it and push down when you do not" — the natural reading is that it starts DOWN on a phone, and that on a laptop (where it is a side pane, not an overlay) starting open costs nothing. Starting it up on a phone is what makes Home unusable on arrival. `experience.v1` §11's subtraction test is the one to apply: the phone shape currently removes the ability to act on the list at all until an undocumented gesture is made.

Likely fix: open the console by default only where it is a pane, not where it is an overlay — i.e. have the client start with `consoleOpen` false below 980px (or below 520px), leaving the toggle exactly as it is.

Not fixed in this lane: `app/static/css/console.css`, `app/static/js/state.js`, `app/static/js/render/console.js` and `app/static/js/main.js` are all outside w8-shell-home's file ownership, so this is written down rather than made.

Meanwhile `app/tests/test_shell_home.py` documents the situation honestly: `_push_the_console_sheet_down()` performs the §6 gesture before driving Home's control at 390, with the measurement and the reason in its docstring, rather than pretending the control is reachable without it.
